Ok, I hacked it in and it resolved that issue.

I replaced

```ts
// Next, check if this is a navigation request for a route. Detect circular
// navigations by checking if the request URL is the same as the index URL.
if (this.adapter.normalizeUrl(req.url) !== this.indexUrl && this.isNavigationRequest(req)) {
    // This was a navigation request. Re-enter `handleFetch` with a request for
    // the URL.
    return this.handleFetch(this.adapter.newRequest(this.indexUrl), context);
}
```

with

```ts
if (
    this.adapter.normalizeUrl(req.url) !== this.indexUrl &&
    this.isNavigationRequest(req)
) {
    return this.scope
        .fetch(req)
        .then((res) => {
            // Application is online - caching the result
            return this.scope.caches
                .open(`${this.prefix}:app:cache`)
                .then((cache) => cache.put(req, res.clone()))
                .then(() => res);
        })
        .catch(() => {
            // Application is offline - try returning the matching result from cache
            return this.scope.caches
                .open(`${this.prefix}:app:cache`)
                .then((cache) => cache.match(req));
        });
}
```

Now the Document is requested all the time, despite the SW cache, when that fails due to being offline the cached version is matched and used. Redirect when online works fine.

